				try {
					const stopReason = e.isAborted ? 'interrupted' : e.finishReason;
					const tokenUsage = await this._getTotalUsage(result);
					const [settledMessage] = settleInterruptedToolParts([e.responseMessage]);
					await chatQueries.upsertMessage({
						...settledMessage,
						chatId: this.chat.id,
```

On top of that, the run already snapshots those same totals onto its own row via `getChatTokenTotals` and `completeRun`, and any compaction during the run is separately recorded as `type: 'compaction'`. So the run's tokens are counted in the usage page today (as chat usage with source `contextRecommendations`).

That matters because `llm_inference` currently holds exactly the inferences that have no `chat_message` to live on — memory extraction, compaction, title generation, and now live story refresh. Nothing reads both tables, so a record here wouldn't corrupt the usage page or budget checks, but it would break that invariant and double-count for anyone summing `v_llm_inference` against chat usage through the admin `query_app_db` tool.

The genuine analogues to what we just did are three services that call `generateText` directly with nothing recording the result: `cron-nlp.ts`, `automation-title.ts`, and `test-agent.service.ts`. Those would each need a `userId` threaded in from their caller, since the column is non-null and they only receive a `projectId`.

I haven't made any changes to context-recommendations yet, so I need to lay out what I've found and ask for direction. The `llm_inference.userId` field is non-nullable, which means cron-nlp, automation-title, and test-agent would all need the user ID threaded through from the tRPC caller—doable but a bigger refactor. I'm leaning toward tracking those direct model calls instead, though I could also add userId to context_rec if needed.
